from h2 import *
A = dict(id="A", levels=L2, kind="Web page", source="Vale Active Travel", numbered=True, title="Leaving the car at home", body=[
 {"p": "Most of us know that driving everywhere is expensive, but few of us realise by how much. Between fuel, insurance, parking and repairs, running a car can cost thousands of pounds a year, and sitting in traffic can eat up hours of your week. Switching even a few journeys to the bus or train could make a real difference to both your wallet and your wellbeing."},
 {"p": "Start by working out what is available. Look at timetables for buses and trains on your usual route, and compare the journey time with driving. You may discover that a quick check saves a lot of faff, and that a stop you had never noticed is only a short walk from your door."},
 {"p": "Next, add up the true cost of each option. A weekly or monthly ticket is usually much cheaper than paying for single journeys, and railcards can reduce fares by a third for those who qualify. Remember to include parking charges when you compare the figures."},
 {"p": "Make the journey as easy as possible. Download a timetable app, keep your ticket on your phone and pack a book or some music. Time on the bus is time you get back, whereas time behind the wheel is time you can never use for anything else."},
 {"p": "Start small. Try leaving the car at home one day a week and see how you get on. Most people find that the first week is the hardest, and that the habit is easier to keep than they feared."},
 {"p": "Finally, keep going. If a service is late or full, do not give up; try a different route or a different time. With a little patience and planning, you will soon find the best way to get around, and you may never look back."}])
B = dict(id="B", levels=L2, kind="Magazine article", source="Transport Weekly", title="Why are we still driving?", body=[
 {"p": "Every weekday morning, millions of people sit alone in their cars, crawling towards offices, schools and shops. A study of 3,000 commuters by the Fenland Travel Panel found that 68 per cent of those who drive to work live within five miles of a bus or train route, yet fewer than one in five had ever tried it."},
 {"p": "So what is stopping them? Many point to unreliable services. If a bus is late twice in a week, most passengers lose faith, and a car offers \"door to door\" convenience that no timetable can match. Others complain that fares rise every year, and that evening and weekend services are too limited to be useful."},
 {"p": "The result is what experts call \"a vicious circle\". Poor services lead to fewer passengers, fewer passengers lead to less money, and less money leads to even poorer services. Breaking the cycle will take investment, but the rewards could be large: less congestion, cleaner air and quieter streets."},
 {"p": "Safety also matters. Passengers who wait for buses in the dark, at stops without shelter or lighting, often feel vulnerable, and some, particularly young people and the elderly, simply choose to stay at home instead. Cost, too, remains a barrier for students and part-time workers, who may spend a large share of their wages on fares."},
 {"box": "**Advice from the Passenger Users' Alliance:** Try your journey once at the weekend, when there is no pressure. Ask your employer about season ticket loans. Report broken lighting and missing shelters to the council. And tell the operator when things go well, as well as when they go wrong."},
 {"p": "There are signs of progress. Several towns have introduced simple, capped fares, and one operator has fitted every bus with free Wi-Fi and charging points. Passenger numbers on these routes have risen by almost a fifth."},
 {"p": "In my view, public transport is the only sensible way to travel into a busy town centre, and councils that ignore it are storing up problems for the future. If you are thinking about changing the way you travel, now is the moment."}])
C = dict(id="C", levels=L2, kind="Transcript of a speech", source="Vale of Fenn community meeting", title="Speech to a public meeting", body=[
 {"p": "Good evening, everyone, and thank you for turning out on such a wet night. I have driven the number 14 for twenty-two years, and I want to tell you why I still believe that public transport is worth fighting for."},
 {"p": "I used to be sceptical myself. When I first joined the company, I thought buses were slow, noisy and old-fashioned. Then I started talking to my passengers. There was a nurse who could not afford a car, a student who read two novels a month on the way to college, and an old gentleman who told me that my bus was the only reason he saw another person all week."},
 {"p": "So what makes a good service? First, it must be reliable, because nobody will wait for a bus that never comes. Second, it must be affordable, because nobody should have to choose between a ticket and a meal. Third, it must be safe, because everyone deserves to feel comfortable at a stop, whatever the hour."},
 {"p": "Will it cost money to get this right? Of course it will. But what is the cost of doing nothing? Empty streets, full car parks and a town centre that nobody can breathe in. I have heard people call a good bus service a luxury. I call it a lifeline for the lonely, a lifeline for the young and a lifeline for the whole town."},
 {"p": "Last year, my route carried more than 400,000 passengers, and 90 per cent of those surveyed said they would recommend it to a friend. Those are not my figures. They are yours."},
 {"p": "I also know about the problems. Buses run late when roadworks block the high street, and shelters are often broken or missing. But when we built a new shelter at the hospital stop, passenger numbers went up within a month. That tells me something simple: people will use a good service if we give them one."},
 {"p": "My advice is this. Try it for a week. Leave the car at home, take a book and see how you feel. You might be surprised at how much more relaxed you arrive, and how much money is left in your pocket at the end of the month."},
 {"p": "I promise you the journey will be worth it. It will be cheaper, greener and, I suspect, a good deal more interesting than another morning in traffic. Thank you, and please, come aboard."}])

